  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis relies on a historical and policy overview rather than a quantitative health or spatial dataset.",
      "what": "This study traces the history of Kuwait's post-oil urban growth to analyze how imported planning frameworks and policy deficits reshaped the built environment. It documents how rapid modernization eradicated major infectious diseases while simultaneously fostering high rates of non-communicable conditions like obesity, diabetes, and cancers, alongside persistent infectious risks for marginalized populations.",
      "why": "It demonstrates how uncritically applying Western urban development paradigms to Middle Eastern contexts can degrade public health and worsen social health equities."
